# Stochastic Processes API Reference

The stochastic-process tools of PlussMa live in `pluss::subsp::math::sde`. They simulate stochastic differential equations over whole `Series` of independent paths, differentiate those simulations with respect to the initial state and the parameters, and integrate against explicit Wiener and jump paths under the Itô, Stratonovich and Marcus conventions.

```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

namespace sde = pluss::subsp::math::sde;
```

Every page of this reference documents one function, enumeration or options structure: what it is, when to call it, every parameter and field, what it returns and what it refuses, and programs with real output that cover each overload and each option.

Looking for the mathematics (why the conventions differ, Itô's lemma, Malliavin calculus)? The {guide}`stochastic processes guide <stochastic>` develops it. The calculus operators that act directly on a `Series` of path samples (`calc.diff.ito`, `calc.antidiff.ito`, the Malliavin derivative) are described there.

## The families

| Family | What it covers |
|---|---|
| [Stochastic differential equations](stochastic/sde.md) | Simulation by Euler-Maruyama, Milstein and Stratonovich-Heun; pathwise gradients by the adjoint method |
| [Stochastic integrals and jump paths](stochastic/integrals.md) | Wiener and jump paths, Itô, Stratonovich and Marcus integrals, adaptedness checking |

## Conventions

- A leading axis of the initial state or the `like` template is a batch of independent paths.
- Noise is elementwise: the diffusion has the shape of the state, and each entry has its own Wiener increment.
- Time is a plain number; the state, the parameters and the paths are `Series`.
- The same seed always gives the same paths.
- Errors are `pluss::Error`; the message names the operation and the problem.

